            {
                id pgparse.oracle
                title "Render-conformance oracle: everything pgorm-query renders must parse under the real PG grammar"
                kind @test
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-02T14:48:56.760729Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Design: dev-dependency on the pg_query crate (pganalyze's libpg_query binding — the actual PostgreSQL server parser). Operator has explicitly waived the C-build-time tax (sole user). Two layers: (1) an assert helper wrapping the render paths in pgorm-query's test suite so every to_string()/build() output must pg_query::parse cleanly — retrofit the existing ~300 render tests through it cheaply (a wrapper on the common assert pattern, not 300 edits); (2) a property layer composing arbitrary builder chains -> render -> must parse. OPEN DECISION at start: version pin — pg_query 5.x speaks PG16 grammar (matches the pgorm-test container), 6.x speaks PG17; pick the conformance target and record it. KNOWN EXPECTED FAILURES the oracle will immediately flag (these are the campaign's bug nodes — mark as known-failing until each lands): WINDOW clause position, ENGINE=/COLLATE= table opts, money(p,s), CREATE PRIMARY KEY INDEX, dangling ON CONFLICT/DO UPDATE SET shapes, Year (panics before render). This node lands BEFORE the renderer campaign — every renderer-touching node now deps on it. Syntax-only ceiling: no catalog, unknown tables/columns pass; the live-PG integration suite remains the semantic oracle."
                    }

                    {
                        at 2026-09-02T16:06:45.622062Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @status
                        text "Doing → Done (nplan complete)"
                    }
                )
            }

            {
                id pgparse.sql-macro
                title "Compile-time sql! macro: raw SQL strings validated by libpg_query at build time"
                deps (pgparse.oracle)
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-09-02T14:49:08.376608Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "The original README aspiration, made real: a macro (working name sql!) that runs libpg_query at compile time over a SQL string literal, so a syntactically invalid raw query is a compile error with the parser's position info spanned onto the literal — the unrepresentable-states principle extended to the raw-SQL escape hatch. Target call sites: SelectorRaw::from_statement, ConnectionTrait::query_raw/execute_raw/batch_execute, migration bodies. Proc-macro crates can't export from pgorm-macros without taxing every build with the C dependency — operator waived the tax, but a sibling crate (pgorm-sql-macro or a feature) is still the cleaner shape; decide at start. Honest ceiling: syntax only — unknown columns/tables still pass; document that the macro validates grammar, not schema. Multi-statement literals validated via pg_query split+parse. New spec rules under a macros. or sql. prefix; deps on pgparse.oracle for the version-pin decision and dependency plumbing."
                    }

